[
    {
        "content": "<p>Hey everyone,</p>\n<p>Does anyone know of a name in the literature for continuous maps between topological spaces which have quasicompact fibers? I can't find a good name for these things anywhere (other than just spelling out the property), but it seems like a natural enough property that somebody must have given it a name at some point</p>",
        "id": 592243332,
        "sender_full_name": "Raphael Douglas Giles",
        "timestamp": 1777627210
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>There is apparently a notion of a <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Perfect_map\">perfect map</a> which is this plus closed and surjective.</p>",
        "id": 592296791,
        "sender_full_name": "Christian Merten",
        "timestamp": 1777646818
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>But how about just calling it <code>HasCompactFibers</code>?</p>",
        "id": 592296842,
        "sender_full_name": "Christian Merten",
        "timestamp": 1777646838
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>There's also <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=IsProperMap#doc\">docs#IsProperMap</a>, for closed continuous maps with compact fibres: <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=isProperMap_iff_isClosedMap_and_compact_fibers#doc\">docs#isProperMap_iff_isClosedMap_and_compact_fibers</a></p>",
        "id": 592389701,
        "sender_full_name": "Ben Eltschig",
        "timestamp": 1777683428
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Properness is indeed a bit stronger, but it should be \"the right notion\" I think. Do you have an example where properness is too strong ?</p>",
        "id": 592433389,
        "sender_full_name": "Anatole Dedecker",
        "timestamp": 1777718805
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>This came out of discussion of this PR <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/26304\">#26304</a>, where we are defining a notion of pushforward for algebraic cycles (though the connection to \"algebraic\" stuff is a bit hard to see just looking at the code because we've abstracted things into a topological context). One can define the pushforward of algebaic cycles with respect to a quasicompact morphism, which is not necessarily proper. The key topological properties we need of quasicompact morphisms are that they are spectral and that they have quasicompact fibers. Of course, you're right that the morphism really should be proper for most applications because you need this if you want that the pushforward preserves rational equivalence, so in some sense properness is indeed \"the right notion\". Still, I think it's useful to have a notion of pushforward that works for more general quasicompact morphisms - for example if you have a quasicompact scheme U and an open immersion from U to a (separated) scheme X, then it might sometimes come up that we want to extend cycles on U to cycles on X. This could be a special case of the pushforward using the more general definition, but not if we impose that the map be proper.</p>",
        "id": 592436211,
        "sender_full_name": "Raphael Douglas Giles",
        "timestamp": 1777721136
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Is the good concept for you \"open immersion then proper\"? A quasi-finite map is open immersion then finite so is this \"quasi-proper\"?</p>",
        "id": 592437705,
        "sender_full_name": "Kevin Buzzard",
        "timestamp": 1777722260
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Maybe it is helpful for context to explain the following: If <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f : X \\to Y</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1076em;\">f</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">:</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0785em;\">X</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\">Y</span></span></span></span> is a quasi-compact morphism of schemes (topologically that is <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=IsSpectralMap#doc\">docs#IsSpectralMap</a>), then it has (quasi)compact fibers (proof: quasi-compact is stable under base change).</p>\n<p>Now, these are all topological properties so one might hope one can translate this into a pure topology question. The underlying topological space of a scheme is \"locally spectral\", i.e. it is <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=PrespectralSpace#doc\">docs#PrespectralSpace</a>, <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=T0Space#doc\">docs#T0Space</a>, <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=QuasiSober#doc\">docs#QuasiSober</a> and is locally <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=QuasiSeparatedSpace#doc\">docs#QuasiSeparatedSpace</a>.</p>\n<p>It is true that if <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f : X \\to Y</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1076em;\">f</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">:</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0785em;\">X</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\">Y</span></span></span></span> is a spectral map between spectral spaces, then it has quasi-compact fibers (proof: It is true for the constructible topology, because that is compact T2 and the claim follows easily from that.)<br>\nOne can relax this to assuming that <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0785em;\">X</span></span></span></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\">Y</span></span></span></span> are only sober, prespectral and quasi-separated (proof: By replacing <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\">Y</span></span></span></span> by an open compact neighbourhood of the point and <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0785em;\">X</span></span></span></span> by its preimage, we may assume <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0785em;\">X</span></span></span></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\">Y</span></span></span></span> are quasi-compact, so it follows from the previous step.)</p>\n<p>It is not clear though how to replace <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=QuasiSeparatedSpace#doc\">docs#QuasiSeparatedSpace</a> by \"locally quasi-separated\"  in the previous paragraph: We may again assume <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0785em;\">X</span></span></span></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\">Y</span></span></span></span> are compact and we may also cover <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0785em;\">X</span></span></span></span> by open quasi-separated compacts <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_i</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8333em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.109em;\">U</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3117em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.109em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">i</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>. But then the composition <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>→</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_i \\to X \\to Y</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8333em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.109em;\">U</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3117em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.109em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">i</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0785em;\">X</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\">Y</span></span></span></span> is no longer quasi-compact if <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0785em;\">X</span></span></span></span> is not quasi-separated.</p>",
        "id": 592437950,
        "sender_full_name": "Christian Merten",
        "timestamp": 1777722425
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Do you really care about non-qcqs schemes?</p>",
        "id": 592438344,
        "sender_full_name": "Kevin Buzzard",
        "timestamp": 1777722655
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Since the AG assumption quasi-compact morphism is sufficient, I don't think imposing unnecessary assumptions for the purely topological part is reasonable only to avoid a slightly unusual assumption like <code>HasCompactFibers</code>.</p>",
        "id": 592438585,
        "sender_full_name": "Christian Merten",
        "timestamp": 1777722855
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I mean, if you're doing a theory of cycles? Sure if we're doing abelian schemes over a base we want to allow the base to be arbitrarily bad because then the representability theorems are stronger. But for a theory of cycles it's usually the case that there are assumptions on everything in sight to make them non-pathological.</p>",
        "id": 592438677,
        "sender_full_name": "Kevin Buzzard",
        "timestamp": 1777722936
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I’ll have to think about this more, but let me just note that our <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=IsProperMap#doc\">docs#IsProperMap</a> does not include separation.</p>",
        "id": 592438712,
        "sender_full_name": "Anatole Dedecker",
        "timestamp": 1777722963
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"110038\">Kevin Buzzard</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/116395-maths/topic/Name.20for.20a.20continuous.20map.20with.20quasicompact.20fibers/near/592438677\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>But for a theory of cycles it's usually the case that there are assumptions on everything in sight to make them non-pathological.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>I agree with this, but I don't think the specific content of the PR (\"pushforward of an algebraic cycle\") needs any of these additional assumptions and they would also not make any proof easier.</p>",
        "id": 592439142,
        "sender_full_name": "Christian Merten",
        "timestamp": 1777723280
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I have been thinking for a while that we should introduce <code>IsQuasiProperMap</code> to mean \"continuous and preimages of compact sets are compact\", because that's the terminology stacks uses for that too - would that work for your application, or is it still too strong?</p>",
        "id": 592476056,
        "sender_full_name": "Ben Eltschig",
        "timestamp": 1777749056
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"646363\">Ben Eltschig</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/116395-maths/topic/Name.20for.20a.20continuous.20map.20with.20quasicompact.20fibers/near/592476056\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I have been thinking for a while that we should introduce <code>IsQuasiProperMap</code> to mean \"continuous and preimages of compact sets are compact\", because that's the terminology stacks uses for that too - would that work for your application, or is it still too strong?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>I think this is still too strong - the main application we want is quasicompact morphisms of schemes, which satisfy that the preimages of open quasicompact sets are quasicompact, and that the fibers are compact. But the preimage of an arbitrary quasicompact set under one of these morphisms may in general fail to be quasicompact</p>",
        "id": 592559227,
        "sender_full_name": "Raphael Douglas Giles",
        "timestamp": 1777815643
    }
]